{
  "rule": "Where each CPython `ast` labelled edge lands in our tree. The node map says the same things are there; this says they are CONNECTED the same way. Two trees can agree on every span and every kind and still attach the children under different names -- `orelse` where `body` belongs is a program and its opposite, with nothing else to tell them apart.",
  "forms": "`[\"right\"]` -- the oracle's child IS the child under our `right` field. `[\"body>\"]` -- it lives SOMEWHERE UNDER our `body` field, which is what a list field looks like when we wrap it: CPython's `ClassDef.body` is a list of statements and ours is one `block` node holding them. The weaker claim is the true one there, and it still checks something. `[]` -- we attach this child POSITIONALLY, with no field name at all.",
  "unlabelled_note": "Roughly half of CPython's labelled edges are `[]` here. That is a finding, not a formality: an unnamed edge is one a consumer cannot query by role. A call's `function` is labelled and its arguments are not; a `return`'s value has no name. Some of those are deliberate (positional lists, and slice bounds where the empty forms have to stay expressible) and some are just gaps. Each entry carries which.",
